  "data": {
    "A": "Research the sparse combinatorial algorithm: deduplicate blocked coordinates, sort them by x+y, and compute each blocked point’s valid arrival count by subtracting contributions from earlier reachable blocked points using binomial path counts. Precompute factorials and inverse factorials modulo 1,000,000,007 up to 2N−2. Include the destination in the recurrence, and handle a blocked start or destination correctly. Measure its correctness, runtime, and memory as obstacle counts vary; account for its O(M²+N) work and do not assume it wins for every valid input.",
    "B": "Research a memory-safe dense fallback using in-place, one-dimensional DP over rows and a sorted obstacle list (or an equally efficient compact lookup). Preserve the baseline recurrence and blocked-cell semantics while reducing storage from O(N²) to O(N+M); its work remains O(N²). Validate edge cases including N=1, blocked endpoints, and duplicate obstacle coordinates. Measure against the baseline and the sparse approach, especially where the sparse recurrence’s O(M²) work is unattractive.",
    "C": "Explore whether parallelism helps the sparse obstacle recurrence on the 4-core ARM64 target. One option is to batch independent blocked points by equal x+y, compute their recurrence sums in parallel, and synchronize between batches. Measure synchronization and thread overhead against a sequential sparse implementation; retain this direction only if end-to-end measurements show a gain. Verify coordinate ordering, duplicate handling, and modular arithmetic across all valid inputs.",
    "G": "Integrate only complete, correct variants from A/B/C. Evaluate a hybrid that selects sparse counting or the linear-memory O(N²) DP based on measured costs, with the choice based on input dimensions and obstacle count rather than particular test values. Re-measure the integrated program independently: gains may not compose. Preserve the required input/output behavior and validate against the oracle on all cases before retaining any optimization.",
    "analysis": "The supplied source is a correct rolling-row recurrence, but still allocates an N×N blocked matrix and performs O(N²) DP. The official constraints allow N up to 199,999, making that storage a major scalability risk. The baseline measurement is correct on 4 cases and takes a mean 0.07927 s (score 0.5) on the stated 4-core ARM64, with no GPU. Sparse obstacle counting can reduce work substantially for small M, but its pairwise recurrence is O(M²), so retain a measured dense fallback rather than assuming it always wins. All approaches must compute results for arbitrary valid inputs, not encode sample or benchmark answers."
